infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title SIGTERM vs SIGKILL: a request to you vs a command to the kernel
  desc Do all cleanup during SIGTERM so SIGKILL never has to fire
  compares
    - label SIGTERM · signal 15
      children
        - label A request to your process
          desc Interrupts the process and runs the handler you registered
        - label Catchable and handleable
          desc Can be caught, blocked, or handled in code
        - label Your one chance to drain
          desc Finish in-flight requests, close connections, flush
        - label Goal: finish here
          desc Complete cleanup during SIGTERM so SIGKILL never fires
    - label SIGKILL · signal 9
      children
        - label A command to the kernel
          desc Never delivered to your code — there is no handler
        - label Uncatchable
          desc Cannot be caught, blocked, or handled
        - label No cleanup, no flush
          desc The kernel destroys the process immediately
        - label In-flight work is lost
          desc Same state as an abrupt exit; fires at the grace-period deadline
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
